Yes. I researched this, and there is an important distinction that will affect the design of your ACH refund/prefund application.

1. The key finding

If Fiserv is sending your bank an ACH-formatted file, the underlying file is very likely based on the standard NACHA 94-character fixed-width format.

A standard ACH file is not simply:

HEADER
TRANSACTION
TRANSACTION
TRANSACTION
TRAILER

It is hierarchical:

File Header (1)
    ↓
Batch Header (5)
    ↓
Entry Detail (6)
    ↓
Optional Addenda (7)
    ↓
Entry Detail
    ↓
...
Batch Control (8)
    ↓
Batch Header (5)
    ↓
...
Batch Control (8)
    ↓
File Control (9)
    ↓
Padding (9)

Each record is exactly 94 characters. The file is normally blocked in groups of 10 records, with 9 records used for padding when necessary. 

However, I could not find a publicly available Fiserv document that establishes a proprietary “Fiserv ACH refund request file” layout for your specific bank/vendor relationship. Fiserv’s public documentation confirms that its ACH products use NACHA-compliant processing and that its PEP+ platform supports prefunding, but institution/vendor-specific transmission layouts can be separately configured. 

So I would not assume that the Fiserv vendor-to-bank pre-fund/refund file is exactly the standard NACHA file until you obtain the bank/Fiserv interface specification.

